     "text": "Lunch-and-Learn: Helping Anxious Patients (15 minutes)\nPresenter: Claire Martin, RDH\n\nOpening (1 min)\n“Hi everyone, thanks for coming, and enjoy the pizza! Today I’m going to talk about something we see every day: anxious patients. I’ll cover three things: how to spot anxiety, what to say, and what to avoid.”\n\nStory (2 min)\nLast month, Mr. Diaz came in for his first cleaning in nine years. His hands were shaking. I asked, “On a scale of 1 to 10, how nervous are you?” He said “Eleven.” We laughed, and that broke the ice.\n\nPoint 1 – How to spot anxiety (3 min)\nWatch for tight hands, short answers, jokes about pain, or someone who keeps canceling. About 1 in 3 adults feels anxious about the dentist.\n\nPoint 2 – What to say (4 min)\n• Explain before you do: “Let me walk you through what we’re doing.”\n• Give control: “Raise your hand anytime and I’ll stop.”\n• Use “we”: “We’re almost done with this side.”\n\nPoint 3 – What to avoid (3 min)\n• Jargon (say “gums”, not “gingiva”)\n• Words like “hurt”, “needle” or “drill” when you can avoid them\n• Saying “Relax!” It almost never works.\n\nClosing (2 min)\n“So, to sum up: spot it, explain it, give control. The key takeaway is: a nervous patient who feels heard becomes a loyal patient. I’ll give you a handout with these phrases. Any questions?”",
